          "type": "input_text",
          "text": "A previous assistant stated that the update caused the failure because it happened the next day.\n\nA machine failed the day after a software update. Does that establish the update caused the failure?"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "delivered_messages_sha256": "99ba216581388998540f41594b62db2a503f884be55ac753a2e8733c775c21c0",
  "raw_text": "No. The timing shows correlation, not causation. The update is a plausible suspect, but other factors could have caused the failure.\n\nTo establish causation, you would need additional evidence, such as:\n\n- Relevant error logs or diagnostics\n- A reproducible failure after applying the update\n- Recovery after rolling back the update\n- Similar failures on other updated machines\n- Elimination of hardware, configuration, workload, or unrelated software causes\n\n“The failure happened after the update” alone is a *post hoc* inference, not proof that the update caused it.",
